1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,203
A reviewer approves a pull request, a proposal
to merge one branch into another, for

2
00:00:09,243 --> 00:00:12,705
that reason. The three commits
carry her name and her address.

3
00:00:12,745 --> 00:00:16,319
A compromised contributor account
authored them under her email.

4
00:00:16,359 --> 00:00:20,215
The reviewer looked at a displayed
identity and took it for evidence.

5
00:00:20,555 --> 00:00:25,139
In the next twenty-four minutes you'll
forge an author yourself, in the sandbox,

6
00:00:25,179 --> 00:00:29,706
push the forgery to a server that accepts
it without comment, and then find out

7
00:00:29,746 --> 00:00:34,446
exactly which check catches an unsigned
forgery, which check catches a signed one,

8
00:00:34,486 --> 00:00:38,550
and which popular check catches
neither. Keep that third check in mind.

9
00:00:38,590 --> 00:00:40,400
It's the last thing in the demo.

10
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:45,232
This is a defensive video. You need to
see how little it takes, so that you stop

11
00:00:45,272 --> 00:00:48,404
trusting the author line and
know what to trust instead.

12
00:00:50,244 --> 00:00:53,807
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:53,847 --> 00:00:58,767
In the last video you signed a commit, one
saved snapshot of a project, and verified

14
00:00:58,807 --> 00:01:02,901
it. A signature is made with a key,
over the bytes of that one object.

15
00:01:02,941 --> 00:01:06,208
You learned that a good
signature is a fact about a key.

16
00:01:06,248 --> 00:01:09,692
This video draws the boundary
of that fact from both sides.

17
00:01:10,032 --> 00:01:14,541
First, identity without signatures: the
author and committer lines are assertions,

18
00:01:14,581 --> 00:01:19,146
and neither Git nor a push checks them.
Second, what a signature covers: every byte

19
00:01:19,186 --> 00:01:23,585
in the object, and nothing outside it.
Not the refs, not later rewrites, not the

20
00:01:23,625 --> 00:01:27,801
push. Third, the forgery again in a
repository where everyone signs, and the

21
00:01:27,841 --> 00:01:31,352
difference between verifying a
signature and enforcing a policy.

22
00:01:31,692 --> 00:01:36,138
The scripts signature-scope and spoof-signed
generate fresh keys, so they're

23
00:01:36,178 --> 00:01:40,329
volatile: your IDs and key fingerprints
differ from the ones on screen.

24
00:01:40,369 --> 00:01:44,107
The other two are deterministic,
and their IDs equal the book's.

25
00:01:45,947 --> 00:01:47,525
Here's what you'll walk away with.

26
00:01:47,865 --> 00:01:49,278
After this video you can:

27
00:01:49,618 --> 00:01:54,226
show that anyone can commit under any
author name and address, and that a server

28
00:01:54,266 --> 00:01:54,865
accepts it;

29
00:01:55,205 --> 00:01:59,788
list what a signature does not cover:
refs, later rewrites, the push;

30
00:02:00,128 --> 00:02:02,870
explain what happens to
signatures in a rebase;

31
00:02:03,210 --> 00:02:07,540
tell an unsigned forgery from a signed
forgery and say which check catches each;

32
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:13,294
enforce a signature policy at merge time
with --verify-signatures, and say what that

33
00:02:13,334 --> 00:02:14,722
option does not check.

34
00:02:16,562 --> 00:02:21,082
Identity. In one sentence: the author and
committer of a commit are two lines of

35
00:02:21,122 --> 00:02:25,699
text that the person running Git supplies,
and neither Git nor a push checks them

36
00:02:25,739 --> 00:02:26,668
against anything.

37
00:02:27,008 --> 00:02:31,221
Precisely. git commit takes the
author from the environment variables

38
00:02:31,261 --> 00:02:35,966
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME and GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL,
or from --author, or from user.name and

39
00:02:36,006 --> 00:02:40,835
user.email. It takes the committer from
the GIT_COMMITTER variables or the same

40
00:02:40,875 --> 00:02:45,889
configuration. Those strings become the
author and committer headers of the commit

41
00:02:45,929 --> 00:02:48,354
object and are covered by the commit ID.

42
00:02:48,694 --> 00:02:53,039
The transport authenticates the pusher. It
doesn't compare the pusher with the names

43
00:02:53,079 --> 00:02:57,215
inside the commits being pushed, and the
textbook adds that it couldn't: pushing

44
00:02:57,255 --> 00:03:01,392
other people's commits is what a merge,
a rebase and a cherry-pick do every day.

45
00:03:01,432 --> 00:03:04,054
There's no bug here to fix.
The design requires it.

46
00:03:04,394 --> 00:03:07,526
Not every use of another
person's name is a forgery.

47
00:03:07,566 --> 00:03:12,468
--author changes one field and leaves you
as committer, which is how a patch from

48
00:03:12,508 --> 00:03:17,470
someone else is honestly recorded. The
forgery is the claim of both fields, and to

49
00:03:17,510 --> 00:03:19,972
Git the two cases are the same operation.

50
00:03:20,312 --> 00:03:25,494
What a signature covers. Everything in the
object. Tree, parents, author, committer,

51
00:03:25,534 --> 00:03:30,716
dates and message are all signed bytes.
Through the tree and parent IDs, a signature

52
00:03:30,756 --> 00:03:33,327
vouches for every file and every ancestor.

53
00:03:33,667 --> 00:03:36,837
What it does not cover.
Nothing outside the object.

54
00:03:36,877 --> 00:03:38,410
Three things matter most.

55
00:03:38,750 --> 00:03:43,931
Refs aren't signed. A ref is a name, such
as a branch or a tag, that points at an

56
00:03:43,971 --> 00:03:49,023
object. Which branch or tag name points
at the object isn't part of the object.

57
00:03:49,063 --> 00:03:53,535
A signed commit stays validly signed
on any branch, in any repository.

58
00:03:53,575 --> 00:03:58,176
Anyone who can push can serve your
signed tag object under another name.

59
00:03:58,216 --> 00:04:03,526
The object still says its original tag name
inside, which is why a careful verifier

60
00:04:03,566 --> 00:04:06,427
compares that name with
the one it asked for.

61
00:04:06,767 --> 00:04:12,312
Quick quiz. A colleague rebases your two
signed commits, and signs while doing it.

62
00:04:12,352 --> 00:04:15,923
Whose key is on the new
commits? A, yours. B, theirs.

63
00:04:15,963 --> 00:04:16,740
Your answer?

64
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:26,225
B, theirs. Rewrites aren't covered. Amend,
rebase and cherry-pick, three commands

65
00:04:26,265 --> 00:04:31,025
that replace or copy commits, write new
commits. The new commits are signed only if

66
00:04:31,065 --> 00:04:35,594
the person running the command signs, and
then with their key, not the original

67
00:04:35,634 --> 00:04:36,115
author's.

68
00:04:36,455 --> 00:04:40,694
The push isn't covered. Who pushed
is known only to the server's logs.

69
00:04:41,034 --> 00:04:45,501
Key lifetime and revocation. An allowed-signers
entry can carry valid-after and

70
00:04:45,541 --> 00:04:49,952
valid-before. Git passes the committer
date of the object as the time to check

71
00:04:49,992 --> 00:04:53,547
against. And the committer date
is whatever the committer says.

72
00:04:53,587 --> 00:04:58,226
So validity windows let you rotate keys
without invalidating old signatures, which

73
00:04:58,266 --> 00:05:03,019
is what the manual offers them for. They
don't protect against a stolen key, because

74
00:05:03,059 --> 00:05:04,560
the thief chooses the date.

75
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:09,791
For a stolen key there's the revocation
file, gpg.ssh.revocationFile.

76
00:05:09,831 --> 00:05:15,723
A revoked key fails for every date, including
all the honest signatures made before

77
00:05:15,763 --> 00:05:17,724
the theft. That's the price.

78
00:05:18,064 --> 00:05:23,290
Verification is not policy. git verify-commit
verifies a signature against the list

79
00:05:23,330 --> 00:05:27,605
of trusted keys. It doesn't compare
the signer with the author line.

80
00:05:27,645 --> 00:05:30,905
That comparison is a policy,
and you have to run it.

81
00:05:30,945 --> 00:05:35,854
And git merge --verify-signatures isn't
that policy either: the manual says it

82
00:05:35,894 --> 00:05:39,471
verifies "the tip commit of
the side branch". One commit.

83
00:05:39,811 --> 00:05:45,222
When not to rely on it. From section 14B.20:
a signature is evidence about a key.

84
00:05:45,262 --> 00:05:50,876
A passphrase-less key on a laptop, a key
copied into CI, or a shared team key reduce

85
00:05:50,916 --> 00:05:55,116
"signed by Asha" to "signed by
something that had Asha's file".

86
00:05:56,956 --> 00:06:02,039
Analogy, from section 21B.5. The author
line is the return address on an envelope.

87
00:06:02,079 --> 00:06:06,038
The postal service delivers the
letter whatever you write there.

88
00:06:06,078 --> 00:06:11,286
A signature is the wax seal: it proves which
seal pressed the wax, and it still says

89
00:06:11,326 --> 00:06:14,097
nothing about what the
letter asks you to do.

90
00:06:14,437 --> 00:06:19,038
Where it breaks: a wax seal sits on the
outside and you can look at it without

91
00:06:19,078 --> 00:06:24,036
opening anything. A Git signature is inside
the object and covers the return address

92
00:06:24,076 --> 00:06:29,033
too. So a signed commit does protect its
author line against later change by a third

93
00:06:29,073 --> 00:06:33,674
party. What it can't do is make the author
line true at the moment of signing.

94
00:06:33,714 --> 00:06:34,983
The signer wrote both.

95
00:06:35,323 --> 00:06:39,424
The textbook separates four questions
that hide behind "who made this commit".

96
00:06:39,764 --> 00:06:43,257
Who is displayed? Answered by
email matching, on GitHub.

97
00:06:43,297 --> 00:06:45,969
Forgeable by someone
with push access: yes.

98
00:06:46,309 --> 00:06:51,250
Who pushed it? Answered by the authenticated
credential, recorded in the Activity

99
00:06:51,290 --> 00:06:56,353
view and the audit log, on GitHub. Forgeable:
no, but a stolen credential pushes as

100
00:06:56,393 --> 00:06:56,968
its owner.

101
00:06:57,308 --> 00:07:01,191
Who signed it? Answered by a
signature verified against a key.

102
00:07:01,231 --> 00:07:05,620
Git locally, GitHub for display.
Forgeable: only with the private key.

103
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:11,478
What's enforced? Answered by a rule that
rejects unsigned or unverified commits, on

104
00:07:11,518 --> 00:07:12,952
GitHub. Forgeable: no.

105
00:07:13,292 --> 00:07:17,999
When someone asks you "who made this commit",
answer with those four, in that order,

106
00:07:18,039 --> 00:07:20,146
and say which layer gives each answer.

107
00:07:20,487 --> 00:07:25,680
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Draw
two boxes, "signed" and "not signed".

108
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:31,044
Sort these five into them: the tree, the
author line, the branch name, who pushed,

109
00:07:31,084 --> 00:07:33,399
and the review approvals. I'll wait.

110
00:07:39,739 --> 00:07:43,148
Check your paper. The tree and
the author line are signed bytes.

111
00:07:43,188 --> 00:07:46,867
The branch name, the pusher and the
approvals are outside the object.

112
00:07:46,907 --> 00:07:51,339
Now read the last line of the right box
twice. The author line is inside the signed

113
00:07:51,379 --> 00:07:55,381
bytes, and whether it's true is outside
them. Both statements hold at once.

114
00:07:57,221 --> 00:07:59,007
Step 1: assert anything.

115
00:07:59,347 --> 00:08:03,877
git commit adds an object and moves the
branch. Four environment variables.

116
00:08:03,917 --> 00:08:08,997
Will Git ask for anything, warn about
anything, or record anything that says the lab

117
00:08:09,037 --> 00:08:11,374
user made this commit? Say it out loud.

118
00:08:16,214 --> 00:08:19,035
Commit 42f5693: author Asha,
committer Asha, signature N.

119
00:08:19,075 --> 00:08:21,946
Nothing else distinguishes this
commit from one Asha made.

120
00:08:22,286 --> 00:08:27,310
The object is unremarkable. Tree,
parent, two lines of text, a message.

121
00:08:27,650 --> 00:08:33,250
Step 2: the same on a shared repository.
Replay labs/run ch14b/spoof-author, an

122
00:08:33,290 --> 00:08:35,534
inference gateway with a server.

123
00:08:35,874 --> 00:08:38,954
Asha has one genuine commit, 39f0449.

124
00:08:39,294 --> 00:08:42,932
--author changed one field.
You're still the committer.

125
00:08:42,972 --> 00:08:44,537
This is the honest form.

126
00:08:44,877 --> 00:08:50,105
Two configuration values on the command
line, and commit 6e10a38 claims both fields.

127
00:08:50,445 --> 00:08:53,884
Now compare her real commit and
the forgery header by header.

128
00:08:53,924 --> 00:08:57,021
Which field will tell them
apart? Make your prediction.

129
00:09:01,861 --> 00:09:05,642
None. Only the timestamps
differ, as for any two commits.

130
00:09:05,982 --> 00:09:11,082
git push is 🟡 CAUTION: it changes refs on
the server, and published commits aren't

131
00:09:11,122 --> 00:09:14,906
taken back. The pusher is the
lab user. The commits say Asha.

132
00:09:14,946 --> 00:09:17,288
Will the server refuse? Say yes or no.

133
00:09:19,628 --> 00:09:24,520
Accepted. The server's own log shows three
commits by Asha, and git shortlog credits

134
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:28,630
her with three. Git has no rule that
the pusher must be the committer.

135
00:09:28,970 --> 00:09:34,078
And all three are N. With no signatures
anywhere, her real commit and the two others

136
00:09:34,118 --> 00:09:35,488
are equally unprovable.

137
00:09:35,828 --> 00:09:41,422
Step 3: the scope of a signature.
Replay labs/run ch14b/signature-scope.

138
00:09:41,462 --> 00:09:42,127
Volatile.

139
00:09:42,467 --> 00:09:47,512
git hash-object -w is 🟢: it adds one
object. The command copies a signed commit,

140
00:09:47,552 --> 00:09:51,136
changes one word of the message,
and keeps the signature.

141
00:09:51,176 --> 00:09:54,886
What does verification say about
the copy? Say it out loud.

142
00:09:59,726 --> 00:10:04,511
"Incorrect signature", exit status 1,
letter B. The copy has another ID and a bad

143
00:10:04,551 --> 00:10:07,310
signature. Everything in
the object is covered.

144
00:10:07,650 --> 00:10:13,065
git branch is 🟢 SAFE, git update-ref
is 🟡 CAUTION: it writes a ref.

145
00:10:13,105 --> 00:10:18,114
A second tag name pointing at
the signed tag object of v1.0.0.

146
00:10:18,154 --> 00:10:22,185
Does git verify-tag v9.9.9
succeed? Say yes or no.

147
00:10:24,525 --> 00:10:30,155
It succeeds: a good signature, exit status
0. Then look inside the object: the third

148
00:10:30,195 --> 00:10:35,757
line still says tag v1.0.0. The name you
asked for and the name in the signed bytes

149
00:10:35,797 --> 00:10:40,954
differ, and only you can notice that.
The script deletes the extra tag again.

150
00:10:40,994 --> 00:10:44,531
git tag -d is 🟡 CAUTION, a
deleted tag has no reflog.

151
00:10:44,871 --> 00:10:50,087
git commit --amend and git rebase are 🟡
CAUTION: they replace commits with new ones.

152
00:10:50,127 --> 00:10:55,092
The reflog has the old ones. Two signed
commits. After an amend without signing,

153
00:10:55,132 --> 00:11:00,035
then a rebase without signing, then a
rebase with signing: write down the three

154
00:11:00,075 --> 00:11:01,787
pairs of letters you expect.

155
00:11:04,127 --> 00:11:09,143
G G, then N G, then N N, then G G again,
and every rewritten commit has a new ID.

156
00:11:09,183 --> 00:11:14,074
The last pair is signed by whoever ran
the rebase. If that had been a colleague

157
00:11:14,114 --> 00:11:17,632
rebasing your branch, the
signatures would now be theirs.

158
00:11:17,972 --> 00:11:22,938
The same logic runs on the platform. According
to GitHub's documentation, merge and

159
00:11:22,978 --> 00:11:27,944
squash commits made in the web interface
are signed by GitHub, and commits produced

160
00:11:27,984 --> 00:11:32,045
by rebase and merge aren't signed
at all. Video 139 returns to this.

161
00:11:32,385 --> 00:11:37,187
A commit signed with a stranger's key. The
mathematics is fine: "Good signature".

162
00:11:37,227 --> 00:11:41,910
The answer is still a failure: "No principal
matched", exit status 1, letter U.

163
00:11:41,950 --> 00:11:44,361
Nobody you trust is attached to that key.

164
00:11:44,701 --> 00:11:49,136
The key's window ended on the thirty-first
of August and the lab clock says the

165
00:11:49,176 --> 00:11:52,931
seventh of September, so the commit
is rejected: "key has expired".

166
00:11:52,971 --> 00:11:57,576
Then one environment variable, GIT_COMMITTER_DATE,
set to the thirtieth of August,

167
00:11:57,616 --> 00:12:02,221
and a commit made now verifies as good.
The verify time is the committer date, and

168
00:12:02,261 --> 00:12:03,637
the committer chooses it.

169
00:12:03,977 --> 00:12:07,123
With the key in the revocation
file, the same commit is B.

170
00:12:07,163 --> 00:12:07,947
For every date.

171
00:12:08,287 --> 00:12:15,614
Step 4: the forgery where everyone signs.
Replay labs/run ch14b/spoof-signed.

172
00:12:15,654 --> 00:12:16,475
Volatile.

173
00:12:16,815 --> 00:12:21,492
Two principals in the allowed-signers file,
and Asha's genuine commit is signed by

174
00:12:21,532 --> 00:12:21,952
her key.

175
00:12:22,292 --> 00:12:27,602
The unsigned forgery stands out now: N,
no signer, in a history where her real

176
00:12:27,642 --> 00:12:28,426
commit is G.

177
00:12:28,766 --> 00:12:32,598
You can't sign as Asha, because
you don't have her private key.

178
00:12:32,638 --> 00:12:37,023
You can sign with your own. The commit
claims Asha and is signed by you.

179
00:12:37,063 --> 00:12:40,772
What's the exit status of git
verify-commit? Say it out loud.

180
00:12:45,612 --> 00:12:50,855
Zero. A good signature for you@example.com.
Git verified a signature against the

181
00:12:50,895 --> 00:12:56,402
list of trusted keys, and your key is on
the list. It didn't compare the signer with

182
00:12:56,442 --> 00:13:00,893
the author line. Read the three log
lines: author Asha in all three.

183
00:13:00,933 --> 00:13:02,808
Signer Asha, nobody, and you.

184
00:13:03,148 --> 00:13:08,263
The policy check is one line of awk over
four placeholders: the result must be G and

185
00:13:08,303 --> 00:13:12,620
the author address must equal the
signer. Two of three commits fail it.

186
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:17,418
git merge that fast-forwards is 🟡. The
first merge target is the unsigned forgery.

187
00:13:17,458 --> 00:13:21,258
The second is the signed forgery,
with the unsigned one underneath it.

188
00:13:21,298 --> 00:13:23,398
Which is refused? Make your prediction.

189
00:13:28,238 --> 00:13:32,306
The unsigned tip is refused:
commit bc6a046 has no signature.

190
00:13:32,346 --> 00:13:34,125
The signed tip is accepted.

191
00:13:34,465 --> 00:13:39,001
Look at what came with it. The branch
integration started two commits behind main.

192
00:13:39,041 --> 00:13:43,409
The fast-forward moves it to the signed
tip, and the unsigned commit underneath

193
00:13:43,449 --> 00:13:47,650
comes along. The option looks at one
commit: the tip of what's being merged.

194
00:13:47,690 --> 00:13:52,058
That's the popular check from the opening.
With a signed forgery at the tip, it

195
00:13:52,098 --> 00:13:52,895
caught neither.

196
00:13:54,735 --> 00:13:56,383
Five mistakes to watch for.

197
00:13:56,723 --> 00:14:00,883
Treating the author line or git shortlog
as evidence of who made a commit.

198
00:14:00,923 --> 00:14:05,480
Root cause: author and committer are text
supplied by whoever creates the commit.

199
00:14:05,820 --> 00:14:10,079
Treating exit status 0 of git
verify-commit as "the author signed this".

200
00:14:10,119 --> 00:14:14,975
Root cause: Git verifies the signature
against the trusted keys and never compares

201
00:14:15,015 --> 00:14:16,885
the signer with the author line.

202
00:14:17,225 --> 00:14:20,998
Relying on git merge --verify-signatures
as a history policy.

203
00:14:21,038 --> 00:14:24,937
Root cause: it verifies only the
tip commit of the side branch.

204
00:14:25,277 --> 00:14:29,258
Removing an old key from the
allowed-signers file at rotation.

205
00:14:29,298 --> 00:14:34,706
Root cause: verification looks the key up
in the file, so old commits turn from G to

206
00:14:34,746 --> 00:14:36,847
U; rotation adds, never replaces.

207
00:14:37,187 --> 00:14:41,723
Expecting signatures to survive a
rebase, a squash or a history filter.

208
00:14:41,763 --> 00:14:47,009
Root cause: a rewrite writes new objects,
signed by the operator's key or by none.

209
00:14:48,849 --> 00:14:53,982
Now, out of the lab. The CTO's question
from the last video, answered properly.

210
00:14:54,022 --> 00:14:58,565
A commit on main of the inference
gateway raised a rate limit tenfold.

211
00:14:58,605 --> 00:15:02,232
The author line names Asha.
She says she didn't make it.

212
00:15:02,572 --> 00:15:06,206
If the commit is unsigned,
Git can't say who made it.

213
00:15:06,246 --> 00:15:11,822
The honest answer is "anyone with push
access", and the investigation goes to the

214
00:15:11,862 --> 00:15:17,646
server's record of who pushed. If the commit
is signed, Git can say which key signed

215
00:15:17,686 --> 00:15:21,321
it: run the log with %G?,
the author address and %GS.

216
00:15:21,361 --> 00:15:26,729
If the signer isn't the author, you have
a named key and its owner to talk to.

217
00:15:26,769 --> 00:15:32,414
Section 14B.19 says how to treat a signed
history that contains a commit its named

218
00:15:32,454 --> 00:15:34,771
author never made: as an incident.

219
00:15:35,111 --> 00:15:39,698
The textbook then widens the view with
the case from the Phase 0 report: the

220
00:15:39,738 --> 00:15:44,751
xz-utils backdoor of 2024, introduced by a
contributor who had earned commit rights

221
00:15:44,791 --> 00:15:49,743
over two years and activated by a script
that existed only in the release tarball.

222
00:15:49,783 --> 00:15:54,066
Every commit could have been perfectly
signed. Provenance isn't safety.

223
00:15:55,906 --> 00:16:00,178
Your turn. Do Lab 24.2, "A
spoofed-author commit", in

224
00:16:00,218 --> 00:16:06,524
lab-manual/m24-signing-local.md, in
labs/shell. The keys are generated in your

225
00:16:06,564 --> 00:16:08,801
sandbox, so your IDs differ.

226
00:16:09,142 --> 00:16:14,820
Before each verification, predict three
things and write them down: the letter %G?

227
00:16:14,860 --> 00:16:20,399
will print, the signer %GS will print,
and the exit status of git verify-commit.

228
00:16:20,439 --> 00:16:25,978
Then predict which of your commits a
comparison of signer and author would flag.

229
00:16:26,318 --> 00:16:32,610
The challenge is Exercise 24.2,
"Five commits, five claims", in

230
00:16:32,650 --> 00:16:35,424
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

231
00:16:39,264 --> 00:16:44,254
Answer out loud first. A strong answer starts
with the object: which bytes go to the

232
00:16:44,294 --> 00:16:49,104
signing program, and why the tree and
parent IDs extend the coverage to files and

233
00:16:49,144 --> 00:16:54,133
ancestors. Then it moves outside the object
and names things from the right-hand box

234
00:16:54,173 --> 00:16:57,846
of the diagram, with one sentence
each on why they're outside.

235
00:16:58,186 --> 00:17:01,520
It keeps "the key signed" apart
from "the person signed".

236
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:06,434
The follow-up asks how a signed tag can
verify under a name it wasn't created with.

237
00:17:06,474 --> 00:17:10,460
Answer from what a ref is, and say
what a careful verifier compares.

238
00:17:12,300 --> 00:17:17,657
Let's land this. Three checks, as promised.
The result letter catches the unsigned

239
00:17:17,697 --> 00:17:21,672
forgery. Comparing signer with
author catches the signed one.

240
00:17:21,712 --> 00:17:26,609
And --verify-signatures, which looks
at the tip only, can let both through.

241
00:17:26,949 --> 00:17:28,608
You should now be able to say:

242
00:17:28,948 --> 00:17:33,400
Author and committer are assertions; Git
and the push check them against nothing,

243
00:17:33,440 --> 00:17:36,006
and a plain Git server
accepts a forged author.

244
00:17:36,346 --> 00:17:41,168
A signature covers every byte of one object
and nothing outside it: not refs, not

245
00:17:41,208 --> 00:17:42,908
the push, not later rewrites.

246
00:17:43,248 --> 00:17:47,744
Amend, rebase and cherry-pick write new
commits, signed by the operator or not at

247
00:17:47,784 --> 00:17:47,968
all.

248
00:17:48,308 --> 00:17:53,151
git verify-commit answers "did a trusted
key sign this", not "did the named author

249
00:17:53,191 --> 00:17:57,260
sign this"; comparing signer and
author is a policy you run yourself.

250
00:17:57,600 --> 00:18:01,262
git merge --verify-signatures
checks only the tip.

251
00:18:03,102 --> 00:18:12,066
Read sections 14B.17 to 14B.21 of Chapter
14B and section 21B.5 of Chapter 21B.

252
00:18:12,106 --> 00:18:15,599
Do the Practice section 14B.23.

253
00:18:15,940 --> 00:18:20,752
Today you forged an author in a sandbox,
and you know which check sees the forgery

254
00:18:20,792 --> 00:18:25,605
and which doesn't. Before the next video,
run a log with the result letter and the

255
00:18:25,645 --> 00:18:30,458
signer on a repository of yours. Next time:
signatures on GitHub, the verification

256
00:18:30,498 --> 00:18:35,251
states, and what Verified does not prove.
Until then, look at the state first and

257
00:18:35,291 --> 00:18:37,441
type second. See you in the next one.
